Historical Example: The Portland Building
Another prominent historical example of Postmodernism is The Portland Building in Portland, Oregon,
designed by architect Michael Graves and completed in 1982.
 Significance: It is widely considered the first major built work of Postmodern architecture in the public sector, 
 marking a decisive break from the sleek, glass-and-steel skyscraper aesthetic of Corporate Modernism.
 Characteristics: Graves utilized a vibrant, non-traditional color palette (terracotta, turquoise, and cream) and 
 integrated heavy classical motifs on a monumental scale—such as stylized pilasters, massive figurative garlands,
 and abstract decorative keystones. It rejected the functional "box" in favor of historical symbolism, ornamentation, 
 and visual wit.
Source & Further Reading
 Architectural Analysis: Heinrich Klotz, The History of Postmodern Architecture (MIT Press, 1988) — An extensive historical documentation of the shift away from orthodox modernism, featuring deep analyses of Michael Graves's municipal projects and the theoretical debates surrounding the Portland Building.

